// NEGATIVE TEST — the PARITY half of
// tests/reject/field_shorthand_needs_a_struct_variant.pd (N6).
//
// That fixture refuses `M::Pair { x, y }` on a TUPLE variant; this one refuses
// `M::Pair { x: a, y: b }`, the same mistake written out in full. The pair is
// what licenses the sentence "shorthand adds no reach" at this boundary: the
// brace form is refused on a tuple variant however it is spelled, so the
// shorthand did not create the error and removing the shorthand would not
// remove it.
//
// The refusal is the TYPE CHECKER's, not the parser's — the parser accepts
// `path "::" identifier '{' … '}'` for any path, because at that point it does
// not know what the path names. The decision is made on the SHAPES alone (a
// braced pattern against a positional variant); the field list is never
// examined, which is why the two spellings cannot diverge. Measured here rather
// than argued, for the reason its sibling states.
enum M {
    Pair(i64, i64),
    Quit,
}

fn main() {
    let v = match M::Pair(1, 2) {
        M::Pair { x: a, y: b } => a + b,
        M::Quit => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
